<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net11.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <!-- SDK default-item globs otherwise treat output from nested projects as source
         content. A project named `tests`, for example, can copy tests/bin and
         tests/obj back into its own output on every incremental build. Exclude
         build artifacts at every depth, not only this project's root bin/obj. -->
    <DefaultItemExcludes>$(DefaultItemExcludes);**/bin/**;**/obj/**</DefaultItemExcludes>
    <!-- NU1510: .NET 11 SDK package-pruning advisory ("PackageReference will not be pruned").
         Several kernel packages carry redundant direct references already provided transitively;
         NuGet 6.12+ surfaces NU1510 for each. Suppress globally rather than per-package to
         keep TreatWarningsAsErrors effective for all other warnings. Cleanup of the redundant
         references is tracked separately. -->
    <!-- NETSDK1206: YDotNet.Native (the Yjs CRDT binding used by kernel-crdt) ships a
         distribution-specific linux-x64-musl RID asset that .NET 8+ no longer resolves from the
         default RID graph. The fleet only ever builds osx / win / linux-*-gnu RIDs (never musl —
         see the Harborline App sidecar RID table), so the musl asset is irrelevant and the advisory is
         pure noise that TreatWarningsAsErrors turns into a build failure (e.g. the osx-x64 sidecar
         publish). Suppress globally; revisit only if a musl/Alpine target is ever added. -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NU1510;NETSDK1206</NoWarn>
    <!-- NETSDK1206 is an SDK-targets advisory (not a C# compiler warning), and NoWarn does not
         reliably suppress its *emission* — so also demote it to a build message. Together these
         give a genuinely clean (0-warning, 0-error) build under TreatWarningsAsErrors. -->
    <MSBuildWarningsAsMessages>$(MSBuildWarningsAsMessages);NETSDK1206</MSBuildWarningsAsMessages>
    <!--
      harborline-api addition (ticket 008 Phase A step 7), not present in the source root.
      The NoWarn above states the intent that NU1510 is suppressed repo-wide, but a number of
      the copied projects set a bare <NoWarn>CS1591</NoWarn> that does NOT include $(NoWarn), so
      they discard the inherited suppression. That count was 36 when this comment was written
      and is 28 today: seven acquired $(NoWarn) in later commits and one project was deleted.
      Do not treat the number as current without re-counting — the register is still open.
      Four of them reference a prunable package and therefore fail restore under
      TreatWarningsAsErrors. The source hit this too and patched
      it per-project twice (foundation-scheduling and blocks-migration-erpnext re-add NU1510
      by hand), which is a fix that has to be repeated for every future project that trips it.
      WarningsNotAsErrors is immune to a project-level NoWarn override, so it enforces the
      already-stated global intent once. This does NOT silence NU1510 — it stays a visible
      warning; it only declines to fail the build on an advisory the source chose to suppress.
      The bare-NoWarn projects are a source defect: cleanup register C-004.
    -->
    <!-- HLQ.CS.1000 / VSTHRD002 and HLQ.CS.1100 / CA1031 are Preview-mode
         evidence for CQG. Keep these two warnings visible in ErrorLog without
         weakening warning-as-error handling for any other analyzer rule. -->
    <WarningsNotAsErrors>$(WarningsNotAsErrors);NU1510;VSTHRD002;CA1031</WarningsNotAsErrors>
    <Authors>Harborline Software Contributors</Authors>
    <Company>Harborline Software</Company>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Apache-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/Harborline-Software/shipyard</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/Harborline-Software/shipyard.git</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    ADR 0164 D6 — versions are DERIVED FROM GIT, never hand-maintained.

    This block replaced a repo-wide literal <Version>0.1.0</Version>. That single line was the
    version of 167 packable projects at once, which is the exact opposite of the "independently
    versioned" the ADR requires: no package could move without moving all of them, and the two
    projects that needed their own version had already hand-overridden it and drifted.

    MinVer computes the version from the nearest reachable git tag. An artifact's version is then
    a FACT ABOUT THE COMMIT that produced it rather than something somebody remembered to bump —
    which is the property that makes a published package trustworthy at all.

    Untagged history yields a 0.0.0-alpha.0.N prerelease, so a build without tags still produces a
    monotonic, obviously-prerelease version rather than silently claiming 0.1.0 forever.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <MinVerTagPrefix>v</MinVerTagPrefix>
    <!-- Bump the MINOR of the next version by default; a release tag states the real number. -->
    <MinVerAutoIncrement>minor</MinVerAutoIncrement>
    <!--
      Per ADR 0164 D7 (amended 2026-08-03), PRERELEASE IS THE DEFAULT.
      The repository default remains alpha; the local NuGet beta pipeline overrides its
      prerelease identifiers on the command line.
      An untagged or between-tags build mints -alpha, so a STABLE version can only come from a
      deliberate stable tag. That inverts the risk: the accident is a prerelease nobody installs
      by default, rather than a 1.0.0 somebody depends on. npm will not resolve a prerelease from
      an ordinary range and NuGet needs an explicit opt-in, so consumption is opt-in by
      construction rather than by permission.
    -->
    <MinVerDefaultPreReleaseIdentifiers>alpha.0</MinVerDefaultPreReleaseIdentifiers>
  </PropertyGroup>


  <!--
    Plan 2 Task 4.3 — auto-wire the HARBORLINE_API_I18N_001 analyzer (Harborline.Api.Analyzers.LocComments)
    into every project that follows the standard Resources/Localization/*.resx convention.
    Projects without that folder pay no cost; projects with .resx bundles get build-time
    enforcement that every <data> entry carries a non-empty <comment>.

    Why ProjectReference (not PackageReference): keeps the analyzer in lockstep with its
    in-repo source — no publish-then-consume round trip. OutputItemType="Analyzer" +
    ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" means consumers take no runtime dependency; only the
    Roslyn compiler loads the analyzer DLL.

    Severity is Warning by default (per the analyzer's DiagnosticDescriptor); Plan 5 (CI
    Gates) promotes to Error after Phase 1 cascade. Repo-wide TreatWarningsAsErrors=true
    means it already fails the build today on any missing comment — which is the point.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\Resources\Localization')
                        And '$(MSBuildProjectName)' != 'Harborline.Analyzers.LocComments'">
    <ProjectReference Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)packages\analyzers\loc-comments\Harborline.Analyzers.LocComments.csproj"
                      OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
    <AdditionalFiles Include="$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\Resources\Localization\*.resx" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    HARBORLINE_API_I18N_002 — auto-wire the Harborline.Api.Analyzers.LocUnused analyzer onto every project
    that follows the Resources/Localization/*.resx convention. Mirrors the LocComments wiring
    above (same predicate, same OutputItemType="Analyzer", same ReferenceOutputAssembly="false").

    Note: AdditionalFiles for *.resx is already declared by the LocComments ItemGroup above,
    and Roslyn shares AdditionalFiles across all analyzers in the compilation, so we don't
    re-declare it here (would cause duplicate-item warnings).

    Severity is Error by default (per the analyzer's DiagnosticDescriptor — promoted in PR #124).
    Repo-wide TreatWarningsAsErrors=true is therefore irrelevant for this rule; it fails the
    build on any unused .resx <data> entry that has no localizer["Key"] / .GetString("Key") match
    in the same package's compilation.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\Resources\Localization')
                        And '$(MSBuildProjectName)' != 'Harborline.Analyzers.LocUnused'">
    <ProjectReference Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)packages\analyzers\loc-unused\Harborline.Analyzers.LocUnused.csproj"
                      OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ADR 0013 enforcement gate — auto-wire the Harborline.Analyzers.ProviderNeutrality
    analyzer onto every project under apps/*, packages/blocks-*, and packages/foundation-*
    so that references named in the analyzer's BannedVendorNamespaces.txt declaration
    fail the build there. Mirrors the LocComments / LocUnused auto-wiring pattern
    above (same OutputItemType="Analyzer", same ReferenceOutputAssembly="false").

    Predicate is computed up front into _HarborlineApiProvNeutTarget so the ItemGroup
    condition stays readable. Targets:
      - apps/*   (matched via the project path, independent of assembly naming)
      - packages/blocks-*/   (matched via project name "Harborline.Blocks.*")
      - packages/foundation-*/ + packages/foundation/   (project name starting with "Harborline.Foundation")
    Excluded:
      - Harborline.Foundation.Integrations — this IS the contract seam where vendor-
        neutral integration interfaces live (ADR 0013); it must not reference vendor
        SDKs but it would otherwise match the foundation-* path predicate.
      - The analyzer project itself (no self-attach).
      - Any test project (project name ends in ".Tests" — test fixtures may use
        vendor SDK mocks if needed).
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <_HarborlineApiProvNeutRelativeProjectPath>$([MSBuild]::MakeRelative('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)', '$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)'))</_HarborlineApiProvNeutRelativeProjectPath>
    <_HarborlineApiProvNeutTarget Condition="'$(_HarborlineApiProvNeutTarget)' == ''">false</_HarborlineApiProvNeutTarget>
    <_HarborlineApiProvNeutTarget Condition="$([System.String]::Copy('$(_HarborlineApiProvNeutRelativeProjectPath)').StartsWith('apps\')) Or $([System.String]::Copy('$(_HarborlineApiProvNeutRelativeProjectPath)').StartsWith('apps/'))">true</_HarborlineApiProvNeutTarget>
    <!-- MSBuildProjectName is the stable csproj basename here; AssemblyName is not available when .props imports. -->
    <_HarborlineApiProvNeutTarget Condition="$(MSBuildProjectName.StartsWith('Harborline.Blocks.'))">true</_HarborlineApiProvNeutTarget>
    <_HarborlineApiProvNeutTarget Condition="$(MSBuildProjectName.StartsWith('Harborline.Foundation'))">true</_HarborlineApiProvNeutTarget>
    <_HarborlineApiProvNeutTarget Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'Harborline.Foundation.Integrations'">false</_HarborlineApiProvNeutTarget>
    <_HarborlineApiProvNeutTarget Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'Harborline.Analyzers.ProviderNeutrality'">false</_HarborlineApiProvNeutTarget>
    <_HarborlineApiProvNeutTarget Condition="$(MSBuildProjectName.EndsWith('.Tests'))">false</_HarborlineApiProvNeutTarget>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(_HarborlineApiProvNeutTarget)' == 'true'">
    <ProjectReference Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)packages\analyzers\provider-neutrality\Harborline.Analyzers.ProviderNeutrality.csproj"
                      OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    ADR 0095's Step 3 bootstrap analyzer was wired here and has been removed.
    See harborline-migration docs/adr/0005: signal-bridge does not become a
    repository, so ADR 0095's Step 1 and Step 2 substrate never arrives. Neither
    IBootstrapContext nor MapBootstrapEndpoints existed anywhere in this
    repository outside the analyzer's own source, so both of its diagnostics
    were unfireable. If pre-tenant signup is ever authored here, the
    confused-deputy seam returns with it and the analyzer should return
    alongside the substrate rather than ahead of it.
  -->

  <!--
    ADR 0013 enforcement gate (Phase 3 / BannedSymbols layer) — auto-wire the
    Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.BannedApiAnalyzers package + the solution-root
    BannedSymbols.txt onto every packageable, non-test, non-analyzer project.
    Diagnostic RS0030 fires (and fails the build, given repo-wide
    TreatWarningsAsErrors=true) when source references any banned symbol.

    BannedSymbols.txt is intentionally empty in v0 — the wiring is what ships.
    Specific bans are added in follow-up commits as landmines surface (e.g.
    "ban System.Web.HttpUtility everywhere — even in providers-*").

    Predicate: every project in the repo — packages/, apps/, src/, hosts/,
    eng/canary/ and tooling/ alike — EXCEPT
      - tests projects (MSBuildProjectName ends in .Tests, or named "tests"),
      - analyzer projects (these target netstandard2.0 with their own version pins
        and opt-out of central package management),
      - the BannedSymbols.txt itself.

    Only the membership predicate lives here. The PackageReference itself is wired
    in Directory.Build.targets, because it must also test
    ManagePackageVersionsCentrally — a property projects set in their own body,
    which .props is imported too early to observe. Same constraint as MinVer; see
    the ADR 0164 D6 note in that file.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <_HarborlineBannedApiTarget Condition="'$(_HarborlineBannedApiTarget)' == ''">true</_HarborlineBannedApiTarget>
    <_HarborlineBannedApiTarget Condition="$(MSBuildProjectName.EndsWith('.Tests'))">false</_HarborlineBannedApiTarget>
    <_HarborlineBannedApiTarget Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'tests'">false</_HarborlineBannedApiTarget>
    <_HarborlineBannedApiTarget Condition="$(MSBuildProjectName.StartsWith('Harborline.Analyzers.'))">false</_HarborlineBannedApiTarget>
    <!-- Also exclude any project whose name ends in `.Analyzers` (e.g.
         Harborline.Wayfinder.Analyzers per W#42 P3b). Future analyzer projects
         under any namespace inherit this exclusion automatically. -->
    <_HarborlineBannedApiTarget Condition="$(MSBuildProjectName.EndsWith('.Analyzers'))">false</_HarborlineBannedApiTarget>

    <!-- ADR 0085 D4 / HLQ.CS.1000 and HLQ.CS.1020: attach the upstream VS
         threading analyzer to every production project while keeping tests and
         analyzer projects outside the measured population. -->
    <_HarborlineThreadingAnalyzersTarget Condition="'$(_HarborlineThreadingAnalyzersTarget)' == ''">true</_HarborlineThreadingAnalyzersTarget>
    <_HarborlineThreadingAnalyzersTarget Condition="$(MSBuildProjectName.EndsWith('.Tests'))">false</_HarborlineThreadingAnalyzersTarget>
    <_HarborlineThreadingAnalyzersTarget Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'tests'">false</_HarborlineThreadingAnalyzersTarget>
    <_HarborlineThreadingAnalyzersTarget Condition="$(MSBuildProjectName.StartsWith('Harborline.Analyzers.'))">false</_HarborlineThreadingAnalyzersTarget>
    <_HarborlineThreadingAnalyzersTarget Condition="$(MSBuildProjectName.EndsWith('.Analyzers'))">false</_HarborlineThreadingAnalyzersTarget>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Plan 2 Task 4.4 — auto-wire the XLIFF MSBuild tasks (Harborline.Tooling.LocalizationXliff)
    onto every project that follows the standard Resources/Localization/*.resx convention.

    Only the ProjectReference (build-order forcing) lives here in .props. The property
    override (_HarborlineLocXliffTaskAssembly) and the Import of the .targets need
    $(Configuration) which isn't set until after the SDK loads — so those bits live
    in Directory.Build.targets at the repo root.
  -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildProjectDirectory)\Resources\Localization')
                        And '$(MSBuildProjectName)' != 'Harborline.Tooling.LocalizationXliff'
                        And '$(MSBuildProjectName)' != 'Harborline.Tooling.LocalizationXliff.Tests'">
    <ProjectReference Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)tooling\Harborline.Tooling.LocalizationXliff\Harborline.Tooling.LocalizationXliff.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    harborline-api release-envelope properties, carried forward from this repository's own
    Directory.Build.props when the local-node-host build system was merged in (ticket 008
    step 0.5). Appended so MSBuild last-wins gives the envelope precedence on the packaging
    metadata it owns. TargetFramework is deliberately NOT carried: the root is net11.0 (the
    incoming closure inherits it) and the five pre-existing projects pin net10.0 themselves.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <Version Condition="'$(Version)' == ''">0.1.0-preview.1</Version>
    <Authors>Harborline Software Contributors</Authors>
    <Company>Harborline Software</Company>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>Apache-2.0</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/Harborline-Software/harborline-api</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/Harborline-Software/harborline-api.git</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
    <ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(CI)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    ContinuousIntegrationBuild above turns on DeterministicSourcePaths, which PathMaps the
    repository root to the literal "/_/" in every compile-time-embedded string. That is correct
    and required for reproducible NuGet output, but [CallerFilePath] is one of those embedded
    strings, and 22 tests in apps/local-node-host/tests resolve their scan root from it. Under
    CI=true they look for source at '/_/apps/local-node-host/...', which exists on no disk:

      InvalidOperationException : MTW-00B probes could not locate the node-host project root
      from '/_/apps/local-node-host/tests/Identity/Mtw00BRedFixtures/Mtw00BAuthorityProbes.cs'

    This is NOT a platform difference. Measured on this Windows machine, same commit, same SDK:
    3 failures with CI unset, 25 with CI=true. GitHub Actions sets CI=true on every step, so
    every run of the packages workflow has been red on it since the suite acquired those tests.

    Scoped to test projects by name rather than to non-packable projects generally: apps and
    hosts are published artifacts and should keep deterministic paths. Test assemblies are never
    distributed, and they are the only projects that legitimately need to find source on disk.
    The two name predicates match the repo's existing convention (see the analyzer wiring above);
    the local-node-host suite is literally named "tests".

    This unblocks those tests rather than fixing them; [CallerFilePath] remains a compile-time
    constant that will break again in any cross-machine build. The durable fix is to resolve the
    root at runtime by walking up from AppContext.BaseDirectory to Harborline.Api.slnx, which
    NavigationPermissionProjectionArchTests already does correctly in this same suite.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="$(MSBuildProjectName.EndsWith('.Tests')) Or '$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'tests'">
    <DeterministicSourcePaths>false</DeterministicSourcePaths>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="/" Visible="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- The repository .globalconfig (the threading rules outside the ADR 0085 preview set stay advisory)
       is not discovered from a project two directories down in every checkout shape: a detached worktree
       nested under another worktree (the landing's shape) compiled without it and promoted VSTHRD103 to
       error. Include it explicitly for every project (340 s1). -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <GlobalAnalyzerConfigFiles Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory).globalconfig" Condition="Exists('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory).globalconfig')" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
